<p>Hi there, thanks for the crit.</p>
<p>So in a nutshell, the piece <em>should</em> have plenty of clues as to the ontological nature of the &quot;bizarro reality&quot;, and the identity of the Great Benefactor / Wayfarers / Celestium etc. And I do think that it explains the goals of this organisation, its relationship with the &quot;Bastion&quot;, and why Silas is so angry.</p>
<p>Once the reader gets that, I think a lot of the finer details become less tangential. But I do realise that if people aren't getting the <em>nature</em> of the world in the first place, it’s moot.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Spoilers</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>The bizarro reality is <strong>our world</strong>.</li>
<li>Paul and Silas are evangelical Christians, and also SCP fans.</li>
<li>The SS is a Christian extracurricular taken to an unhealthy extreme.</li>
<li>The Bastion is the nation in which they live.</li>
<li>When Silas vents his anger on the wiki, he’s using codewords.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Let me know if you think it needs to be more obvious / if there are other potential snags - thanks!</p>

